<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sofia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Serdica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sofia is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, at the foot of Mount Vitosha. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Serdica</a> it was a Thracian and then Roman city, where Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> issued an edict of toleration for Christians in 311. Taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Krum</a> in 809, it was a Bulgarian, Byzantine and from 1382 Ottoman city, and it was chosen as the capital of the new Bulgarian state in 1879.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sofia.jpg" alt="Sofia" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Deensel · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panoramic_view_over_central_Sofia_and_the_Vitosha_Mountain_2017-10-08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
